The three primitives target different parts of the context problem, so they can be layered. [Claude Code(opens in new tab)](https://code.claude.com/docs/en/memory) is a real-world example that employs compaction alongside [two complementary memory systems(opens in new tab)](https://code.claude.com/docs/en/memory#claudemd-vs-auto-memory): `CLAUDE.md` files hold user-defined instructions and rules (coding standards, project architecture, workflows) that the developer writes and checks into source control; auto memory holds learnings and patterns Claude writes itself (build commands, debugging insights, preferences discovered from corrections).

