  # Giving AI Agents Permanent Memory with MCP and CRDTs
## The Technologies Enabling This Approach
Two technologies, used together, make this architecture practical today. The Model Context Protocol (MCP) solves the interface problem — how an agent talks to its memory. Conflict-free Replicated Data Types (CRDTs) solve the state problem — how concurrent writes merge without loss. Either alone is insufficient. Together they form the substrate.

1. **Model Context Protocol (MCP)**

2. **Conflict-free Replicated Data Types (CRDTs)**

If MCP answers _how agents talk to memory_, CRDTs answer _how memory stays consistent when many agents write to it at once_.

The classical approach to concurrent writes is "last write wins": each update carries a timestamp, and in conflict the newest one overwrites the rest. It is simple, and in a multi-agent world it is quietly destructive. Two agents that legitimately add different items to a set will see one of the additions vanish. The system looks consistent — every replica agrees — but the agreement was bought by throwing valid data away.

CRDTs take a different approach. Given those, "last write wins" is no longer the only tool available; every valid update can contribute, and the result is still deterministic.

CRDTs come in two flavors. Production libraries — Yjs, Automerge, and Loro — typically implement operation-based variants with efficient binary encodings and compaction.

For agent memory, the useful building blocks are:

This is what makes CRDTs well-suited to shared agent memory — but only when the primitive matches the data. LWW semantics on a set silently loses concurrent additions. OR-Set semantics on a counter produces nonsense. "Use a CRDT" is not a design decision; _which_ CRDT, for which slice of memory, is the design decision. Get that mapping right and the system feels coherent. Get it wrong and it feels arbitrary in ways that are hard to debug later.

## How the Architecture Works
![Denis Ermakov's image-e33a08](https://hackernoon.imgix.net/images/JtDUIzo7cjXNpJEmf9gljPemf3o1-jea3bfu.png?auto=format%2Ccompress&w=1920)

The agent never sees the CRDT.

Consider two coding-assistant agents working with the same developer — one on their laptop, one on their work desktop. The laptop agent writes preferences.typescript.strict = true at timestamp T1. The desktop agent writes the same fact at T2. No coordinator, no lock, no lost update.

Propagation is where most naive "distributed memory" designs fall apart. The choice is a deployment decision, not a protocol one.

## Practical Considerations in Implementation
A second consideration is size. CRDTs retain metadata — tombstones for removed elements, causal history for ordering — and that metadata grows monotonically unless you compact it. Yjs and Automerge both ship compaction, but compaction requires a _causal cutoff_: a point beyond which no older operations will be accepted. In a memory system where agents can go offline for days, the cutoff has to be chosen carefully. Too loose and memory grows without bound.

CRDTs converge, which means any replica eventually sees every update it has access to. That is a feature for collaborative editing and a liability for memory. Not every agent should see every fact. Scoping is the privacy model.

## Managing Memory Over Time
CRDTs resolve _syntactic_ conflicts — two writes to the same key. They do not resolve _semantic_ conflicts — two writes that contradict each other in meaning. If one agent records "user prefers Python" and another records "user prefers Go," the CRDT will happily keep both. Resolving which is currently true is the agent's job, not the data structure's. In practice this means a reconciliation pass, either rule-based or LLM-driven, that runs over memory at write time or query time.

Effective memory is not accumulation. It is continuous curation — writing, decaying, consolidating, and occasionally forgetting. The CRDT substrate guarantees the bits merge. The agent layer has to guarantee the meaning stays coherent.

## Comparison to Existing Memory Systems
| Dimension | Centralized (Mem0, Zep, Letta) | Decentralized (MCP + CRDT) |
|-|-|-|
| Multi-agent write | Serialized through service | Concurrent, merged |

