## Memory: write and select beyond the window
Compaction bounds a single task; memory extends the agent across tasks and sessions.

Working memory is the active window, the current step's scratchpad.

Rendering diagram…

View source

Figure 7. The memory hierarchy. The model works in a small, fast working memory and pages to larger, slower tiers: episodic notes for what happened, semantic stores for durable knowledge. The context engine, or the model itself, moves information between tiers.

The Manus team reports treating the file system as externalized context, letting the agent write intermediate artifacts to files and read them back, which keeps the window small while preserving unlimited durable state, and reports that the single most important production metric for them is the key-value cache hit rate, which file-based, append-only context helps protect [[16]](https://www.tmls.nyc/research/context-engineering#ref-manus). The general principle is that anything the agent might need later but does not need now belongs in memory, not in the window.

## Isolation: partitioning context across agents
The fourth operation is isolation, and sub-agents are its main instrument. Instead of one agent accumulating every subtask's tool output in a single growing window, an orchestrator delegates each subtask to a sub-agent with a clean context, and only the distilled result returns to the parent [[19]](https://www.tmls.nyc/research/context-engineering#ref-anthropic-multiagent). Anthropic's multi-agent research system is the canonical example, with parallel subagents each exploring in their own context window and an orchestrator synthesizing the results [[19]](https://www.tmls.nyc/research/context-engineering#ref-anthropic-multiagent).

## A reference architecture for a context engine
The builder selects from episodic and semantic memory and a retriever, compresses via a compactor, orders for cache stability, and returns a budgeted working set.

## Case studies in context engineering
The first is coding agents, where compaction is most visible.
